{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.12","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"colab":{"provenance":[]},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"nvidiaTeslaT4","dataSources":[],"dockerImageVersionId":31329,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":true}},"nbformat_minor":5,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"id":"title","cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Assignment-2: Implementing and Analyzing Variational Autoencoder (VAE)\n\n**Course:** CS772  \n**Submission:** (1) this completed `.ipynb` notebook, and (2) a separate PDF containing all derivations and written answers. The PDF has to be uploaded on **Gradescope** and the notebook has to be uploaded as a **zip file** on the following **Dropbox** link: https://www.dropbox.com/request/br97unrk59kal356hnpf","metadata":{"id":"title"}},{"id":"background","cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Background and Notation\n\nWe observe data $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^D$ and posit a latent variable model:\n\n$$p_\\theta(x, z) = p_\\theta(x|z)\\, p(z)$$\n\nwhere $p(z) = \\mathcal{N}(0, I)$ is a fixed standard normal prior and $p_\\theta(x|z)$ is a learned decoder. We want to maximize $\\log p_\\theta(x)$, but the integral $\\int p_\\theta(x|z)\\,p(z)\\,dz$ is intractable.\n\nThe VAE (Kingma & Welling, 2013) introduces a learned approximate posterior $q_\\phi(z|x) = \\mathcal{N}(\\mu_\\phi(x),\\, \\mathrm{diag}(\\sigma^2_\\phi(x)))$, whose parameters are output by an encoder network. Applying Jensen's inequality yields the **Evidence Lower BOund (ELBO)**:\n\n$$\\log p_\\theta(x) \\;\\geq\\; \\underbrace{\\mathbb{E}_{q_\\phi(z|x)}[\\log p_\\theta(x|z)]}_{\\text{Reconstruction term}} \\;-\\; \\underbrace{D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\!\\left(q_\\phi(z|x) \\,\\|\\, p(z)\\right)}_{\\text{Regularization term}} \\;=:\\; \\mathcal{L}(\\theta, \\phi; x)$$\n\nTraining maximizes the ELBO jointly over $\\theta$ and $\\phi$.\n\n> **Notation:** The encoder outputs two $d$-dimensional vectors: $\\mu$ (mean) and $\\log\\sigma^2$ (log-variance `logvar`).","metadata":{"id":"background"}},{"id":"setup-header","cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## Setup","metadata":{"id":"setup-header"}},{"id":"setup","cell_type":"code","source":"import torch, random\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.nn.functional as F\nimport torch.optim as optim\nfrom torchvision import datasets, transforms\nfrom torch.utils.data import DataLoader\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nSEED = hash(\"PML\")%2**32\nrandom.seed(SEED); np.random.seed(SEED)\ntorch.manual_seed(SEED); torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(SEED)\n\nDEVICE     = torch.device(\"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\")\nBATCH_SIZE = 128\nZ_DIM      = 10\nEPOCHS     = 20\nLR         = 1e-3\n\ntransform     = transforms.ToTensor()   \ntrain_dataset = datasets.MNIST('./data', train=True,  download=True, transform=transform)\ntest_dataset  = datasets.MNIST('./data', train=False, download=True, transform=transform)\ntrain_loader  = DataLoader(train_dataset, batch_size=BATCH_SIZE, shuffle=True)\ntest_loader   = DataLoader(test_dataset,  batch_size=BATCH_SIZE, shuffle=False)\nprint(f\"Using device: {DEVICE}\")","metadata":{"id":"setup","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-02T16:42:52.733514Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-02T16:42:52.733826Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.006648Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-02T16:42:52.733789Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.005857Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stderr","text":"100%|██████████| 9.91M/9.91M [00:00<00:00, 41.4MB/s]\n100%|██████████| 28.9k/28.9k [00:00<00:00, 1.20MB/s]\n100%|██████████| 1.65M/1.65M [00:00<00:00, 9.91MB/s]\n100%|██████████| 4.54k/4.54k [00:00<00:00, 9.41MB/s]","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stdout","text":"Using device: cuda\n","output_type":"stream"},{"name":"stderr","text":"\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":1},{"id":"arch-header","cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## Model Architecture\n\nThe encoder and decoder are defined as separate modules. The `VAE` class composes them and implements the reparameterization trick.","metadata":{"id":"arch-header"}},{"id":"encoder","cell_type":"code","source":"class Encoder(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, z_dim=Z_DIM):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(784, 512),\n            nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(512, 256),\n            nn.ReLU(),\n        )\n        self.fc_mu     = nn.Linear(256, z_dim)\n        self.fc_logvar = nn.Linear(256, z_dim)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        h = self.net(x.view(-1, 784))\n        return self.fc_mu(h), self.fc_logvar(h)\n","metadata":{"id":"encoder","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.008841Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.009291Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.014665Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.009264Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.013983Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":2},{"id":"decoder","cell_type":"code","source":"class Decoder(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, z_dim=Z_DIM):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.net = nn.Sequential(\n            nn.Linear(z_dim, 256),\n            nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(256, 512),\n            nn.ReLU(),\n            nn.Linear(512, 784),\n            nn.Sigmoid()\n        )\n\n    def forward(self, z):\n        return self.net(z).view(-1, 1, 28, 28)\n","metadata":{"id":"decoder","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.015542Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.015783Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.024098Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.015761Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.023434Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":3},{"id":"vae","cell_type":"code","source":"class VAE(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self, z_dim=Z_DIM):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.encoder = Encoder(z_dim)\n        self.decoder = Decoder(z_dim)\n\n    def reparameterize(self, mu, logvar):\n        std = torch.exp(0.5 * logvar)\n        eps = torch.randn_like(std)\n        return mu + eps * std\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        mu, logvar = self.encoder(x)\n        z          = self.reparameterize(mu, logvar)\n        recon_x    = self.decoder(z)\n        return recon_x, mu, logvar, z\n","metadata":{"id":"vae","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.025201Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.025525Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.03808Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.025503Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.03732Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":4},{"id":"part1-header","cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## Part 1\n\n### Q1.1  Reconstruction Loss (10 pts)\n\nAssume the decoder models $p_\\theta(x|z) = \\mathcal{N}(f_\\theta(z), \\sigma^2 I)$, where $f_\\theta(z)$ is the decoder output and $\\sigma^2$ is a fixed constant.\n\n1. Show algebraically that maximizing $\\log p_\\theta(x|z)$ under this Gaussian assumption is equivalent to minimizing the Mean Squared Error (MSE) between $x$ and $f_\\theta(z)$.*\n\n2. In practice we implement a deterministic decoder. The network outputs only the mean $f_\\theta(z)$ and the variance is ignored. Briefly explain what this implies about the quality of VAE-generated images.\n\n3. An alternative is to model each pixel as an independent Bernoulli: $p_\\theta(x|z) = \\prod_i \\text{Bern}(x_i;\\, \\hat{x}_i)$. What loss function does this correspond to? Why might this be more principled for MNIST than MSE?\n\n### Q1.2  Closed-Form KL Divergence (10 pts)\n\nGiven $q_\\phi(z|x) = \\mathcal{N}(\\mu, \\mathrm{diag}(\\sigma^2))$ and $p(z) = \\mathcal{N}(0, I)$, derive the following closed-form expression starting only from the definition of KL divergence:\n\n$$D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\!\\left(q_\\phi(z|x) \\,\\|\\, p(z)\\right) = -\\frac{1}{2}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}\\left(1 + \\log \\sigma_j^2 - \\mu_j^2 - \\sigma_j^2\\right)$$\n","metadata":{"id":"part1-header"}},{"id":"part2-header","cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## Part 2  Implementation\n\n### Q2.1  Reparameterization (5 pts)\n\nImplement `reparameterize` in the `VAE` class above and explain why this trick is necessary. What goes wrong if you write `z = torch.distributions.Normal(mu, sigma).sample()` and try to backpropagate.","metadata":{"id":"part2-header"}},{"id":"q22-header","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Q2.2  Loss Function (10 pts)\n\nImplement the VAE loss below. Use **MSE** for the reconstruction term and the **closed-form KL** from Q1.2.\n\n> **Reduction:** use `.sum(dim=1).mean()` — sum over pixels and latent dimensions, then average over the batch.","metadata":{"id":"q22-header"}},{"id":"vae-loss","cell_type":"code","source":"def vae_loss(recon_x, x, mu, logvar):\n    \n    x_flat     = x.view(x.size(0), -1)\n    recon_flat = recon_x.view(recon_x.size(0), -1)\n    recon_l = F.mse_loss(recon_flat, x_flat, reduction='none').sum(dim=1).mean()\n    kl_l = -0.5 * (1 + logvar - mu.pow(2) - logvar.exp()).sum(dim=1).mean()\n\n    return recon_l, kl_l\n","metadata":{"id":"vae-loss","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.038995Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.039339Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.053747Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.039306Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.053075Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":5},{"id":"q23-header","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Q2.3  Training Loop (5 pts)\n\nImplement the training loop below. Log total loss, reconstruction loss, and KL loss separately each epoch. After training, plot all three on a single figure.","metadata":{"id":"q23-header"}},{"id":"train-loop","cell_type":"code","source":"def train(model, optimizer, loss_fn, loader, epochs=EPOCHS, beta=1.0):\n    model.train()\n    history = {'recon': [], 'kl': [], 'total': []}\n\n    for epoch in range(1, epochs + 1):\n        total_recon = 0.0\n        total_kl    = 0.0\n        total_loss  = 0.0\n\n        for x, _ in loader:\n            x = x.to(DEVICE)\n            optimizer.zero_grad()\n\n            recon_x, mu, logvar, _ = model(x)\n            recon_l, kl_l = loss_fn(recon_x, x, mu, logvar)\n            loss = recon_l + beta * kl_l\n\n            loss.backward()\n            optimizer.step()\n\n            total_loss  += loss.item()    * x.size(0)\n            total_recon += recon_l.item() * x.size(0)\n            total_kl    += kl_l.item()    * x.size(0)\n\n        n = len(loader.dataset)\n        history['recon'].append(total_recon / n)\n        history['kl'].append(total_kl / n)\n        history['total'].append(total_loss / n)\n\n        print(f\"Epoch {epoch:02d}/{epochs} | \"\n              f\"Recon: {history['recon'][-1]:.4f} | \"\n              f\"KL: {history['kl'][-1]:.4f} | \"\n              f\"Total: {history['total'][-1]:.4f}\")\n\n    return history\n","metadata":{"id":"train-loop","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.054624Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.055113Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.067571Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.055079Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.066833Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":6},{"id":"train-run","cell_type":"code","source":"model     = VAE().to(DEVICE)\noptimizer = optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=LR)\nhistory   = train(model, optimizer, vae_loss, train_loader)\n\n# Plot loss curves\nepochs_range = range(1, EPOCHS + 1)\nplt.figure(figsize=(10, 4))\nplt.plot(epochs_range, history['recon'], label='Reconstruction Loss')\nplt.plot(epochs_range, history['kl'],    label='KL Divergence')\nplt.plot(epochs_range, history['total'], label='Total Loss (ELBO)')\nplt.xlabel('Epoch'); plt.ylabel('Loss (per sample avg)')\nplt.title('VAE Training Loss'); plt.legend(); plt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('vae_loss_curves.png', dpi=150)\nplt.show()","metadata":{"id":"train-run","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.069633Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.069848Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-05-02T16:45:26.78832Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-05-02T16:43:04.069806Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-05-02T16:45:26.787632Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Epoch 01/20 | Recon: 43.5904 | KL: 3.4020 | Total: 46.9924\nEpoch 02/20 | Recon: 27.1459 | KL: 7.3761 | Total: 34.5220\nEpoch 03/20 | Recon: 24.3015 | KL: 8.1621 | Total: 32.4635\nEpoch 04/20 | Recon: 22.6394 | KL: 8.7054 | Total: 31.3448\nEpoch 05/20 | Recon: 21.5196 | KL: 9.0596 | Total: 30.5791\nEpoch 06/20 | Recon: 20.7597 | KL: 9.3163 | Total: 30.0760\nEpoch 07/20 | Recon: 20.1632 | KL: 9.5206 | Total: 29.6838\nEpoch 08/20 | Recon: 19.7094 | KL: 9.6268 | Total: 29.3363\nEpoch 09/20 | Recon: 19.3434 | KL: 9.7441 | Total: 29.0875\nEpoch 10/20 | Recon: 19.0243 | KL: 9.8330 | Total: 28.8572\nEpoch 11/20 | Recon: 18.7637 | KL: 9.9158 | Total: 28.6794\nEpoch 12/20 | Recon: 18.5574 | KL: 9.9815 | Total: 28.5389\nEpoch 13/20 | Recon: 18.3511 | KL: 10.0516 | Total: 28.4027\nEpoch 14/20 | Recon: 18.1608 | KL: 10.0951 | Total: 28.2559\nEpoch 15/20 | Recon: 17.9791 | KL: 10.1246 | Total: 28.1037\nEpoch 16/20 | Recon: 17.8648 | KL: 10.1817 | Total: 28.0465\nEpoch 17/20 | Recon: 17.7185 | KL: 10.2309 | Total: 27.9494\nEpoch 18/20 | Recon: 17.6200 | KL: 10.2527 | Total: 27.8727\nEpoch 19/20 | Recon: 17.5302 | KL: 10.2878 | Total: 27.8180\nEpoch 20/20 | Recon: 17.4062 | KL: 10.2918 | Total: 27.6980\n","output_type":"stream"},{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x400 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":7},{"id":"q24-header","cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Q2.4  Monte Carlo KL Approximation (15 pts)\n\nThe KL term can be estimated without a closed form using Monte Carlo (MC) sampling:\n\n$$D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(q_\\phi(z|x) \\| p(z)) = \\mathbb{E}_{q_\\phi(z|x)}\\!\\left[\\log q_\\phi(z|x) - \\log p(z)\\right] \\approx \\frac{1}{L}\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{L}\\left[\\log q_\\phi(z^{(\\ell)}|x) - \\log p(z^{(\\ell)})\\right]$$\n\nwhere each $z^{(\\ell)} \\sim q_\\phi(z|x)$ is drawn using the reparameterization trick.\n\n1. Implement `vae_loss_mc` below. Draw $L$ samples *inside* the loss function. Use `torch.distributions.Normal` for the log-probability evaluations.\n2. Train three fresh models using `vae_loss_mc` with $L \\in \\{1, 10, 100\\}$. Plot the KL loss curves for all three alongside your closed-form baseline.\n3.Describe qualitatively what you observe in the $L=1$ KL curve compared to $L=100$. Why is high variance in this gradient estimate specifically harmful during early training? Why would you prefer the closed form when it is available, even if $L$ is large?","metadata":{"id":"q24-header"}},{"id":"mc-loss","cell_type":"code","source":"def vae_loss_mc(recon_x, x, mu, logvar, L=1):\n    # Flatten inputs\n    x_flat     = x.view(x.size(0), -1)\n    recon_flat = recon_x.view(recon_x.size(0), -1)\n\n    recon_l = F.mse_loss(recon_flat, x_flat, reduction='none').sum(dim=1).mean()\n\n\n    std = torch.exp(0.5 * logvar)                          \n    q   = torch.distributions.Normal(mu, std)\n    p   = torch.distributions.Normal(torch.zeros_like(mu), torch.ones_like(std))\n\n    eps = torch.randn(L, *mu.shape, device=mu.device)        \n    z   = mu.unsqueeze(0) + eps * std.unsqueeze(0)           \n\n    log_q = q.log_prob(z).sum(dim=-1)   # (L, B)\n    log_p = p.log_prob(z).sum(dim=-1)   # (L, B)\n\n    # Average over L samples, then over batch\n    kl_est = (log_q - log_p).mean(dim=0).mean()\n\n    return recon_l, kl_est","metadata":{"id":"mc-loss","trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-05-02T16:45: